//! fail
// An exit that no run reaches keeps its obligation where spec/fir.md 9.5 places it (D17.20). The
// analysis follows each edge of the FIR, also the edge out of `while (true)` that no run takes,
// so in the `void` function `spin` the closing brace of the body is an error. A deferred `del`
// fixes it, as in `spin_freed`. The end of the body of a `noreturn` function is a trap, an abort
// block, so it has no obligation (067).
fn spin() void {
    i32 mut* own n = new(i32);
    while (true) {
    }
} //! error: cannot prove 'n' is empty at this exit

fn spin_freed() void {
    i32 mut* own n = new(i32);
    defer del(n);
    while (true) {
    }
}

fn main() i32 {
    return 0;
}
